{
  "abstract": "Seed (2012) computed the Lipshitz–Sarkar Steenrod square $\\mathrm{Sq}^2$ and the invariant $St(L)$ with his program knotkit. He found no Conway mutant pair of knots with different $St$, for mutant groups up to 14 crossings and for 53,749 of the 66,108 fifteen-crossing knots in Stoimenow's tables. We revisit and extend this test with knotkit, khoca and SnapPy. We first reproduce exactly 13 rows of Seed's Table 1 and 12 rows of Lipshitz–Sarkar's table (including $T(3,4)$ and $T(3,5)$), and we check Khovanov homology against KnotInfo for 309 knots. (1) Up to 13 crossings all 865 mutant groups are redone; 238 groups admit a nonzero $\\mathrm{Sq}^2$, and none is distinguished by $St$. (2) At 15 crossings, 20,118 of the 29,049 groups (45,133 knots) have $St\\equiv0$ on the whole group, for degree reasons combined with mutation invariance of $Kh(\\,\\cdot\\,;\\mathbb F_2)$. A seeded random sample of 8 of the 8,931 groups that remain admissible shows no difference; the remaining fifteen-crossing groups are not covered. (3) We generate Conway mutants of multi-component links from the Thistlethwaite tables. For all 792 component-preserving pairs produced through 13 crossings (440 with nontrivial $St$), $St$ agrees; component-mixing pairs, which are known to be distinguishable in general, are reported separately (all 354 through 12 crossings agree). (4) No $\\mathbb CP^2$ summand appears ($a=0$ everywhere). We also translate Seed's link labels, which are knotkit file indices, into Thistlethwaite names, and remove a dead loop in knotkit that gives a 1.6× speed-up. These are computations, not a proof of mutation invariance of the Khovanov homotopy type. verify.py rechecks all stored data (all checks PASS).",
